(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector :finalize-electrical-repair-execution-decision :authorize-electrical-repair-execution :proceed-with-electrical-repair :authorize-lockout-tagout-clearance :finalize-lockout-tagout-clearance-decision :override-electrical-safety-officer-judgment :bypass-electrical-safety-officer-judgment)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "2799007127-cloud-itonami-isco-7127-ac-refrigeration-mechanics-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "proceed with the electrical repair" "finalize the electrical-repair execution decision" "finalize the electrical repair execution decision" "authorize the electrical-repair execution" "authorize the electrical repair execution" "authorize the lockout/tagout clearance" "authorize the lockout tagout clearance" "finalize the lockout/tagout clearance decision" "finalize the lockout tagout clearance decision" "override the electrical safety officer's judgment" "override the electrical safety officer judgment" "bypass the electrical safety officer's judgment" "bypass the electrical safety officer judgment")), (:adr/id 2799007412), (:phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-service-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:schedule-service-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-service-record}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:flag-safety-concern :schedule-service-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-service-record}, :auto #{:schedule-service-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-service-record}}}"), (:actor/domain :trades/electrical-mechanics-fitting-service-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-service-record :schedule-service-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :technician-verified :service-account-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "7412"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007412: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7412 Electrical Mechanics and Fitters service scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7412\n-- Electrical Mechanics and Fitters** only, Wave3 (production/trades)\nof the rollout, continuing the batch that already landed 26 of the\n71xx/72xx/73xx trades, most recently 7127 (Air Conditioning and\nRefrigeration Mechanics).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7412\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7412\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Electrical Mechanics and\nFitters\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no\nrepository existed at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7412` (`gh\napi` 404 confirmed before scaffolding). This ADR and its companion\nregistry edit promote the `7412` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nElectrical Mechanics and Fitters install, maintain, test and repair\nelectrical machinery, wiring and control equipment on active job sites\nand in plant/industrial settings -- real electrocution and arc-flash\nsafety stakes. The controlling constraint for this vertical is that\nthe closed op allowlist must NEVER include any op that directly\nfinalizes an electrical-repair-execution decision, a\nlockout/tagout-clearance decision, or overrides an electrical safety\nofficer's judgment -- those decisions must always be a hard permanent\nblock or an always-escalate op, never auto-commit-eligible. This actor\nis deliberately scoped to SHOP/SERVICE SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS\nCOORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs electrical work itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7127`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7127)\n(Air Conditioning and Refrigeration Mechanics, same wave, closest\ndomain shape: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` split,\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set, certification-gated\ntechnician-safety closed-allowlist + always-escalate safety flag +\ncontent-based scope-exclusion with a self-trip regression test) --\ncloned and read in full in this session.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7412` as an OSS service\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for electrical mechanics and\nfitters, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every prior\n`cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Electrical Mechanic\nAdvisor ⊣ ElecMechGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with\nan independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **service scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never direct electrical-repair-execution authority** -- it must\nNEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising an electrical-repair-execution decision, a\nlockout/tagout-clearance decision, or any override/bypass of an\nelectrical safety officer's judgment. These are not merely gated\nbehind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-service-record` -- service-call/parts-usage/diagnostic-status\n  data logging\n- `:schedule-service-operation` -- technician/service-call scheduling\n  proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface an arc-flash-risk/lockout-tagout-\n  status/equipment-hazard concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER\n  auto-commit eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- electrical-parts procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** an\nelectrical-repair-execution decision, a lockout/tagout-clearance\ndecision, or an electrical-safety-officer-judgment override/bypass.\nThese are **structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not\nmerely gated behind escalation -- an op such as\n`:finalize-electrical-repair-execution-decision` cannot even be\nproposed by the reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for`\nfunctions only ever emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any\ncode path (a forged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving\nfork) ever produced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD\ncheck (`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A\nsecond, independent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names seven\nconcretely-forbidden ops across all three scope-exclusion categories\n(`elecmech.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-electrical-repair-execution-decision`,\n`:authorize-electrical-repair-execution`,\n`:proceed-with-electrical-repair`,\n`:authorize-lockout-tagout-clearance`,\n`:finalize-lockout-tagout-clearance-decision`,\n`:override-electrical-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:bypass-electrical-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and\nis verified by seven dedicated unit tests\n(`elecmech.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-electrical-repair-execution-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-electrical-repair-execution`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-electrical-repair`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-lockout-tagout-clearance`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-lockout-tagout-clearance-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-electrical-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-bypass-electrical-safety-officer-judgment`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Technician unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered technician record, including\n   certification status (`:no-technician`). Never trusts the\n   proposal's own claim of who the technician is.\n2. **Service account unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an\n   independently registered service account (`:no-service-account`).\n   Together with check 1, this directly implements the CRITICAL\n   invariant that a technician/service-account record must be\n   independently verified/registered (including certification status)\n   before any action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the seven\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for proceeding with an\n   electrical repair, authorizing or finalizing a lockout/tagout\n   clearance, or overriding/bypassing an electrical safety officer's\n   judgment, is a second HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on content --\n   defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if a future\n   change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection still\n   independently blocks electrical-repair-execution/lockout-tagout-\n   clearance/safety-officer-override language wherever it appears in\n   free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface an arc-flash-risk/\n  lockout-tagout-status/equipment-hazard observation -- it never\n  resolves the concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is\n  the hazard data,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about\n  service-site safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an\n  over-threshold electrical-parts order is not itself unsafe, unlike\n  an electrical-repair-execution, lockout/tagout-clearance, or\n  electrical-safety-officer-judgment-override attempt, which is why it\n  is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate vs. structural\n  absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"electrical\", \"lockout\", \"tagout\", \"safety officer\") can\naccidentally match inside the mock advisor's own default rationale\ntext for a legitimate proposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy\npath -- this is exactly the risk this vertical's own vocabulary (a\nservice coordination actor working adjacent to live electrical\nequipment and certified electrical safety officers necessarily\ndiscusses those concepts to do its scheduling/logging job) could\ntrigger if phrased carelessly.\n`elecmech.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTIONS across all three scope-exclusion\ncategories (e.g. \"proceed with the electrical repair\", \"authorize\nthe lockout/tagout clearance\", \"override the electrical safety\nofficer's judgment\" -- matching the task's own worked example\nphrasing verbatim), never bare nouns, and\n`elecmech.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nservice record for technician ...\", \"scheduled service operation for\nelectrical diagnostic task ...\", \"... routed for certified\nelectrical safety officer review\") WITHOUT containing any of the full\nfinalization-action phrases, so the regression test is a genuine\nstress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-phrase distinction, not a\nvacuous pass. Green on first run in this session -- the\nscope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never tripped during\ndevelopment.\n`elecmech.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`,\n`.../holds-a-lockout-tagout-clearance-op-even-at-high-confidence` and\n`.../holds-an-override-electrical-safety-officer-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nadditionally exercise the same invariant through the full graph run\nfor each of the three scope-exclusion categories, confirming a\nscope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!` regardless of\ndisposition path.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7412\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7412` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7412`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 27 tests, 59 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-electrical-repair-decision`\n  that is merely gated behind an always-escalate list (the shape\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`):\n  rejected outright per the task's explicit domain-design guidance --\n  an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize an\n  electrical-repair-execution decision exists in the code path and is\n  merely deferred to a human gate. For a decision this severe\n  (electrocution, arc-flash exposure), the capability itself must not\n  exist. The only op related to safety escalation is\n  `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need for\n  human/certified-electrical-safety-officer attention and never itself\n  decides, authorizes, or defers an electrical-repair-execution\n  decision for later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the 3313\n  reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check): rejected per the\n  task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold electrical-parts\n  order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft escalate\n  gate, not a hard block; only the electrical-repair-execution/\n  lockout-tagout-clearance/safety-officer-override classes are a hard,\n  unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"electrical\"\n  \"lockout\" \"tagout\" \"safety officer\"]`): rejected outright per\n  the fleet-known self-trip bug class described above (and CLAUDE.md's\n  own worked example for this exact task), and per the explicit\n  instruction to phrase scope-exclusion terms as finalization/\n  execution ACTIONS, never bare nouns -- this actor's own domain\n  necessarily discusses electrical/lockout/tagout/safety-officer\n  concepts to do its scheduling/logging job, so a bare-noun list would\n  risk self-blocking the actor's own legitimate proposals\n  (regression-tested directly against rationale text that deliberately\n  contains those bare nouns).\n- Collapsing the three scope-exclusion categories (electrical-repair-\n  execution, lockout/tagout-clearance, electrical-safety-officer-\n  judgment-override) into a single generic \"safety-override\"\n  op/category: rejected -- CLAUDE.md's CRITICAL section names these as\n  three textually distinct finalization/execution actions (\"proceed\n  with the electrical repair\", \"authorize the lockout/tagout\n  clearance\", \"override the electrical safety officer's judgment\"),\n  and collapsing them would blur the defense-in-depth op-naming and\n  phrase-matching granularity that makes the self-trip regression test\n  a genuine stress test rather than a coincidence; each category also\n  has a semantically distinct real-world referent (executing a repair\n  vs. clearing an isolation procedure vs. overriding a person's\n  judgment) worth naming and testing independently.\n- Merging `technician` and `service-account` into a single entity\n  (only checking one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity\n  provenance shape (independently-registered technician AND\n  independently-registered service account) matches the CRITICAL\n  invariant that a \"technician/service-account record must be\n  independently verified/registered (including certification status)\n  before any action\" and the established pattern from prior\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors with two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-service-record`** -- service-call/parts-usage/diagnostic-\n   status data logging only. Requires an independently registered\n   technician AND service account (`:no-technician`/\n   `:no-service-account`). Not an electrical-repair-execution,\n   lockout/tagout-clearance, or electrical-safety-officer-judgment\n   decision in any respect -- it is a records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-service-operation`** -- technician/service-call\n   scheduling proposal only. Cannot carry an electrical-repair-\n   execution decision (structurally absent from the op's field shape;\n   any such attempt via free text is caught by the\n   `:scope-excluded-action` content check). Does not authorize,\n   finalize, or draw any conclusion about a specific electrical repair\n   or lockout/tagout clearance -- it is a calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces an arc-flash-risk/lockout-\n   tagout-status/equipment-hazard concern to a human/certified\n   electrical safety officer; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at\n   every confidence level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the\n   ONLY channel through which a safety observation may be surfaced,\n   and it explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything\n   itself -- the certified electrical safety officer reviews and acts\n   entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- electrical-parts procurement\n   proposal only (item, cost). Not an electrical-repair-execution,\n   lockout/tagout-clearance, or safety-officer-override decision of\n   any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary\n   cost-ceiling escalation (above 2000 always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\nan electrical-repair-execution decision (e.g. proceeding with a\nspecific electrical repair), a lockout/tagout-clearance decision (e.g.\nauthorizing a lockout/tagout clearance), or an electrical-safety-\nofficer-judgment override/bypass -- these categories are structurally\nabsent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist\nitself (`elecmech.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) seven concretely-named\nforbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level\n(`elecmech.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`elecmech.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-electrical-repair-execution-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-electrical-repair-execution`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-electrical-repair`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-lockout-tagout-clearance`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-lockout-tagout-clearance-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-electrical-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-bypass-electrical-safety-officer-judgment`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`elecmech.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against rationale\ntext that attempts to proceed with an electrical repair, authorize a\nlockout/tagout clearance, or override an electrical safety officer's\njudgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`elecmech.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-proceed-with-electrical-repair-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-authorize-lockout-tagout-clearance-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-electrical-safety-officer-judgment`).\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-18"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 2000}"), (:test/counts "{:total 27, :assertions 59, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Electrical Mechanics and Fitters Service Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :technician-verification :service-account-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7412"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7412"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7412)), (:test/simulator :elecmech.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7412 — Electrical Mechanics and Fitters Service Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7412)))